<div class="eyebrow-strip"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Room Two · Modernism · Looking at conventions anew</div>

<section class="page-head">
<div class="sigil-lg" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M20 6 L34 20 L20 34 L6 20 Z"/><path d="M20 15 L29 20 L20 25 L11 20 Z"/></svg></div>
<div>
<h1>Looking At Modern Art</h1>
<p class="sub">When the choices of art become visible</p>
</div>
</section>

<main class="reading">
<p class="lede">Modernist artists often treated inherited artistic conventions not simply as rules to obey or discard, but as choices that could be reconsidered, rearranged, and made visible. Picture space, naturalistic likeness, a unified object, and the frame around an artwork could all become subjects of attention.</p>
<p class="measure">Many of the most distinctive artists of the period share a recurring tendency to thematically investigate the means of art-making itself: marks and surfaces, materials and joins, fragments and titles, display and institutional setting. The four studies in this room begin with a familiar choice from The Looking Studio, then explore what happens artistic convention itself becomes visible as a source of meaning for both artist and audience.</p>
<p class="page-note">The created comparison images in each study are original AI-generated images made with OpenAI GPT Image 2 through Codex, built to isolate one visual relationship at a time. Historical artworks are separately identified and credited where they appear.</p>


<div class="track">
<div class="track-head"><span class="tk-name">Four studies</span><span class="tk-note">Any order · exploration, not progression</span></div>
