<div id="lens-detail" class="lens-detail is-hidden" aria-live="polite">
<div class="detail-bar"><span class="dthread" id="dThread"></span><button class="detail-close" id="dClose" type="button">← All lenses</button></div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="orientation">
<section class="lens" id="lens-orientation" data-screen-label="orientation">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M8 27 L20 16 L32 25 L20 34 Z"/><path d="M14 13 L30 9 L34 22"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">01 · Room Two</span><h2 class="lens-title">Multiple orientations and the picture field</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">When spatial cues no longer all agree. A painting can coordinate familiar things without making every edge, plane, and curve agree about one stable viewing position.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>One still life · two organizations of space</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Look at the table, bowl, bottle, paper, and mandolin in both created images. Notice which edges and curves share a direction and which do not.</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A created image of a cobalt-blue bottle, a terracotta bowl containing three pale pears, folded white paper, and a dark mandolin on a cream tabletop before an ochre wall with a window shadow."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/modern-art/created/modernism-multiple-orientations-coherent-v1.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M0 476 L280 421 M522 356 L890 303 L1536 472" /><path class="guide-line" d="M531 607 C664 546 890 553 1007 621" /><circle class="marker" cx="1210" cy="392" r="26" /><text class="marker-text" x="1210" y="392" style="font-size:44px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="776" cy="559" r="26" /><text class="marker-text" x="776" y="559" style="font-size:44px">2</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A created image of the same cobalt-blue bottle, bowl with three pale pears, folded white paper, and dark mandolin. Angular divisions structure the tabletop and wall around the still life."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/modern-art/created/modernism-multiple-orientations-shifting-v1.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-shape" d="M15 462 L436 300 L681 452 L429 725 Z" /><path class="guide-shape" d="M716 286 L1144 230 L1364 510 L987 614 Z" /><path class="guide-line" d="M288 572 L588 450 M882 707 L1234 545" /><path class="guide-line" d="M1070 685 C1124 638 1225 654 1287 717" /><circle class="marker" cx="423" cy="508" r="26" /><text class="marker-text" x="423" y="508" style="font-size:44px">3</text><circle class="marker" cx="1059" cy="626" r="26" /><text class="marker-text" x="1059" y="626" style="font-size:44px">4</text><circle class="marker" cx="1184" cy="690" r="26" /><text class="marker-text" x="1184" y="690" style="font-size:44px">5</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>On the left, <strong>1 the tabletop boundaries</strong> and <strong>2 the bowl's upper rim</strong> follow a compatible oblique organization. On the right, <strong>3 angular divisions</strong> split the tabletop into local fields; <strong>4 adjacent fields meet along different diagonals</strong>; and <strong>5 the mandolin's curved body remains recognizable</strong> against those diagonals. These are incompatible local directional cues. They do not by themselves establish several literal viewing positions, a rotating object, or elapsed time. They can make the depicted objects and the constructed picture surface noticeable together.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>In this selected comparison, repeated contours, overlaps, intervals, and surface rhythms connect parts of the image even when local directional cues do not resolve into one coherent projection. This is a different spatial organization, not a more advanced stage of seeing.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="orientation-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>Still Life with Apples and Peaches</em></h3><p class="work-meta">Paul Cézanne · c. 1905 · oil on canvas</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="ra-question">When does a still life resist one settled view?</p>
<p class="ra-dek">Cézanne keeps familiar things—fruit, cloth, pitcher, bowl, tabletop—but gives their edges and openings a relationship that does not simply resolve into one stable viewing position.</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Look at the pitcher, the bowl, and the near edge of the table. Which parts seem to show you a surface from above? Which seem to hold their profile?</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" style="aspect-ratio:1920/1541" role="img" aria-label="A whole view of Paul Cézanne's still life with fruit, a pitcher, a bowl, cloth, and a tilted wooden table."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/modern-art/historical/nga-45986-cezanne-still-life-apples-peaches.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1920 1541" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse class="guide-shape" cx="530" cy="575" rx="118" ry="42" /><ellipse class="guide-shape" cx="1322" cy="665" rx="153" ry="50" /><path class="guide-line" d="M861 1057 L1664 915" /><path class="guide-line" d="M1106 1290 L1732 1065" /><circle class="marker" cx="410" cy="535" r="60" /><text class="marker-text" x="410" y="535" style="font-size:90px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1322" cy="590" r="60" /><text class="marker-text" x="1322" y="590" style="font-size:90px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1510" cy="1080" r="60" /><text class="marker-text" x="1510" y="1080" style="font-size:90px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at these relations</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, the pitcher remains broadly in profile while its opening is visible from above. At <strong>2</strong>, the bowl's rim also opens upward. At <strong>3</strong>, the sloping near table edges direct your eye across a surface that seems to tip toward you. The visible relations do not need to prove a literal sequence of viewpoints; they make the represented things and the painted field harder to settle into one uncomplicated projection.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>An artist can keep familiar objects recognizable while reorganizing their orientation. When local edges and openings do not all agree on one stable view, the picture surface itself becomes part of what you notice.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: Paul Cézanne, <em>Still Life with Apples and Peaches</em>, c. 1905, oil on canvas, National Gallery of Art, Washington, 1959.15.1. Digital image: National Gallery of Art, CC0. See the <a href="https://www.nga.gov/artworks/45986-still-life-apples-and-peaches">National Gallery of Art object record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="resemblance">
<section class="lens" id="lens-resemblance" data-screen-label="resemblance">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M9 30 Q19 9 32 15"/><circle cx="25" cy="23" r="3.2"/><line x1="8" y1="10" x2="14" y2="16"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">02 · Room Two</span><h2 class="lens-title">Resemblance and reference</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">When partial likeness, relations, and titles can orient interpretation. A painting can invite associations with a body, an action, or an idea through partial likeness and visual relationships—not only through a fully naturalistic scene.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>Related forms · different degrees of likeness</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Look at the curves, diagonals, repeated colors, and vertical forms in both created images. Notice what associations they invite before trying to name a subject.</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A created image in blue, cream, rust red, ochre, and coral. A large blue form, pale curved forms, a rust-red crescent, a small ochre rectangle, and vertical cream and coral fields fill the composition."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/modern-art/created/modernism-resemblance-figural-cues-v1.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M758 102 C808 154 850 275 889 466" /><path class="guide-line" d="M451 540 C534 462 661 458 747 585" /><path class="guide-line" d="M386 702 C614 839 957 657 1095 268" /><circle class="marker" cx="842" cy="276" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="842" y="276" style="font-size:44px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="555" cy="510" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="555" y="510" style="font-size:44px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="893" cy="612" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="893" y="612" style="font-size:44px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A created image in blue, cream, rust red, ochre, charcoal, and coral. Large curved and diagonal color fields meet in front of vertical cream and coral fields, with a small dark oval near the upper center."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/modern-art/created/modernism-resemblance-relations-v1.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M751 4 C596 69 456 201 394 346" /><path class="guide-line" d="M401 694 C665 827 1010 679 1244 164" /><ellipse class="guide-shape" cx="924" cy="355" rx="142" ry="52" /><circle class="marker" cx="511" cy="166" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="511" y="166" style="font-size:44px">4</text><circle class="marker" cx="861" cy="665" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="861" y="665" style="font-size:44px">5</text><circle class="marker" cx="1040" cy="355" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="1040" y="355" style="font-size:44px">6</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>In the left image, <strong>1 the pale neck-and-shoulder contour</strong>, <strong>2 the bent pale arm</strong>, and <strong>3 the rust-red form crossing the blue torso</strong> support a figure-and-held-form association without identifying a particular person or object. In the right image, <strong>4 a pale arc</strong>, <strong>5 a rust-red crescent crossing a blue field</strong>, and <strong>6 a compact dark oval</strong> echo visible curves, colors, and dark accents in the left image, but their arrangement does not require a complete figure or scene.</p><p class="title-cue">Created title for this pair: <em>Turning</em></p><p>This created title can frame a possible association with movement or a body, but it does not supply a hidden answer to decode.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Partial likeness, recurring visual relations, and a title can orient an interpretation without resolving a painting into one correct subject. Notice how an artist can keep associations open while still giving the viewer something specific to follow.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="resemblance-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>Painting with Green Center</em></h3><p class="work-meta">Vasily Kandinsky · 1913 · oil on canvas</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="ra-question">What can hold an image together when it does not offer a scene?</p>
<p class="ra-dek">Kandinsky's title gives one cue, but the painting asks you to follow color, direction, density, and interval before you decide what, if anything, it depicts.</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Let your eye travel between the green center, the broad red sweep, and the looping dark and pale lines. What relations keep drawing you across the image before you try to name a subject?</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" style="aspect-ratio:2000/1862" role="img" aria-label="A whole view of Kandinsky's abstract painting with a green central mass, a large red sweep, curving lines, and scattered colored patches."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/modern-art/historical/aic-8987-kandinsky-painting-green-center.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 2000 1862" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-shape" d="M500 560 C690 480 895 585 980 765 C1040 930 915 1110 690 1080 C490 1042 395 870 500 560 Z"/><path class="guide-line" d="M810 330 C1120 310 1420 365 1700 560"/><path class="guide-line" d="M185 200 C360 445 260 695 530 950"/><circle class="marker" cx="670" cy="760" r="65"/><text class="marker-text" x="670" y="760" style="font-size:90px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1360" cy="365" r="65"/><text class="marker-text" x="1360" y="365" style="font-size:90px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="330" cy="430" r="65"/><text class="marker-text" x="330" y="430" style="font-size:90px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at the relations that remain visible</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, a dense green mass gathers several neighboring marks. At <strong>2</strong>, the broad red sweep carries your eye across the upper field. At <strong>3</strong>, looping arcs and diagonal strokes interrupt and redirect that movement. The title identifies a green center; it does not supply a hidden scene to decode. Color, direction, and density are already enough to organize your attention.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>Naturalistic likeness is not the only way an artwork can make relations meaningful. Color, line, mass, and interval can establish movement and emphasis even when no familiar object governs the image.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: Vasily Kandinsky, <em>Painting with Green Center</em>, 1913, oil on canvas, Art Institute of Chicago, 1931.510. Digital image: Art Institute of Chicago, public domain/CC0. See <a href="https://www.artic.edu/artworks/8987/painting-with-green-center">the Art Institute of Chicago object record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="construction">
<section class="lens" id="lens-construction" data-screen-label="construction">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><rect x="6" y="11" width="12" height="19"/><rect x="22" y="15" width="12" height="15"/><line x1="18" y1="20" x2="22" y2="20"/><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="1.5" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">03 · Room Two</span><h2 class="lens-title">Parts, joins, and construction</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">When boundaries and attachments remain active in the finished work. A component can remain visible as a component: a boundary, overlap, connector, or attachment can help organize the finished work instead of disappearing into a seamless whole.</p>
<p class="lens-bridge">Building from The Looking Studio's attention to materials and making, this lens shifts from what a work is made of to how distinct parts and their relations remain visible in the finished whole.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>One component vocabulary · two constructed relations</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Look at both created objects. Follow their edges to see where one part stops and another begins. Notice where an overlap or attachment changes the balance of the whole.</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A created freestanding abstract object on a dark rectangular base. A blue vertical plate, rust-red curved plate, pale disk, brass connector, and black rod remain visibly distinct against a warm wall."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/modern-art/created/modernism-construction-freestanding-v1.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse class="guide-shape" cx="480" cy="321" rx="113" ry="132" /><path class="guide-line" d="M567 307 L619 307" /><path class="guide-line" d="M554 500 C712 469 852 404 942 278" /><path class="guide-line" d="M362 259 L402 279" /><path class="guide-line" d="M670 276 L620 305" /><path class="guide-line" d="M836 356 L790 401" /><circle class="marker" cx="330" cy="242" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="330" y="242" style="font-size:44px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="706" cy="257" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="706" y="257" style="font-size:44px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="872" cy="329" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="872" y="329" style="font-size:44px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A created shallow abstract wall construction. A blue rectangular backing panel carries a rust-red curved plate, pale disk with brass connector, and black diagonal rod with dark circular ends."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/modern-art/created/modernism-construction-layered-v1.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M488 735 C730 699 927 475 959 72" /><path class="guide-line" d="M628 391 L682 413" /><circle class="guide-shape" cx="728" cy="881" r="39" /><circle class="guide-shape" cx="986" cy="579" r="39" /><path class="guide-line" d="M615 772 L654 720" /><path class="guide-line" d="M756 334 L681 402" /><path class="guide-line" d="M1114 760 L990 585" /><circle class="marker" cx="580" cy="810" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="580" y="810" style="font-size:44px">4</text><circle class="marker" cx="794" cy="299" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="794" y="299" style="font-size:44px">5</text><circle class="marker" cx="1151" cy="808" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="1151" y="808" style="font-size:44px">6</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>In the left image, <strong>1 the pale disk keeps a clear boundary</strong> against the warm background; <strong>2 the short brass connector and round fastener remain visibly distinct</strong>; and <strong>3 the red curve overlaps the blue plate</strong> while each part keeps its own boundary. In the right image, <strong>4 the red crescent reads as a separate layer</strong> over the blue panel; <strong>5 a small connector remains visible</strong> where the disk approaches the red layer; and <strong>6 the black rod meets the panel at two dark circular end points.</strong> These visible part-relations organize balance and rhythm through boundaries, overlaps, and attachments—not only through the overall silhouette.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>A component, boundary, seam, overlap, or attachment can organize rhythm, balance, and possible associations in the finished work. Notice where one part ends, where another begins, and how their visible relation guides the eye.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="construction-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>Study for a Composition</em></h3><p class="work-meta">Piet Mondrian · 1940–41 · collage, gouache, and charcoal on paper</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="ra-question">When do separate elements become part of the picture's meaning?</p>
<p class="ra-dek">This Mondrian study lets you see red and blue collaged papers, gouache, charcoal, and a pieced paper support as distinct elements in one carefully balanced field.</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Look at the red, blue, and yellow pieces and the dark grid around them. Which edges make the colored areas feel placed on the sheet rather than simply drawn into it?</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" style="aspect-ratio:2000/2844" role="img" aria-label="A whole view of Mondrian's paper study with red and blue collaged papers, a yellow strip, and an extensive charcoal grid."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/modern-art/historical/aic-142570-mondrian-study-composition.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 2000 2844" aria-hidden="true"><rect class="guide-shape" x="180" y="405" width="455" height="342"/><path class="guide-shape" d="M1320 1260 L1695 1260 L1695 1605 L1300 1605 Z"/><rect class="guide-shape" x="1050" y="1965" width="370" height="115"/><path class="guide-line" d="M625 748 L1320 1260"/><circle class="marker" cx="650" cy="570" r="64"/><text class="marker-text" x="650" y="570" style="font-size:90px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1680" cy="1370" r="64"/><text class="marker-text" x="1680" y="1370" style="font-size:90px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1425" cy="2030" r="64"/><text class="marker-text" x="1425" y="2030" style="font-size:90px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at the parts and their boundaries</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, the red field has a sharp edge and a small gap between its two sections. At <strong>2</strong>, blue pieces overlap and meet in an irregular cluster. At <strong>3</strong>, the yellow strip interrupts the surrounding charcoal grid. The Art Institute's object record identifies red and blue papers collaged to the sheet. That source-backed material fact helps you distinguish a documented pasted element from a line that merely resembles a join.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>Parts, edges, and documented material differences can organize a work as actively as a depicted subject. Looking for their placement and relation can show how construction itself shapes what an artwork asks you to notice.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: Piet Mondrian, <em>Study for a Composition</em>, 1940–41, Art Institute of Chicago, 2013.984. Digital image: Art Institute of Chicago, public domain/CC0. See <a href="https://www.artic.edu/artworks/142570/study-for-a-composition">the Art Institute of Chicago object record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="frame">
<section class="lens" id="lens-frame" data-screen-label="frame">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M15 18 h10 v9 a5 5 0 0 1-10 0z"/><path d="M6 6 h6 M6 6 v6 M34 6 h-6 M34 6 v6 M6 34 h6 M6 34 v-6 M34 34 h-6 M34 34 v-6"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">04 · Room Two</span><h2 class="lens-title">Object, frame, and status</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">When setting changes the encounter without exhausting the object's meaning. An object can remain physically recognizable while its setting changes what we notice, what questions we ask, and how it may be treated or interpreted.</p>
<p class="lens-bridge">Building from The Looking Studio's attention to setting, this lens expands the idea of setting from physical placement to the frames that can shape an object's encounter and interpretation.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>One object · two frames of attention</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Look at both created images. Notice which details tell you that the object is the same, and what in each surrounding scene changes what you attend to.</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A created image of a cobalt-blue enamel mug with a cream rim and rust-orange handle on a worn wooden worktable. A tan cloth and shallow wash basin appear nearby."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/modern-art/created/modernism-object-frame-use-v1.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M792 778 C802 743 837 732 861 756" /><ellipse class="guide-shape" cx="769" cy="562" rx="104" ry="89" /><path class="guide-line" d="M0 558 L389 685" /><circle class="marker" cx="860" cy="812" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="860" y="812" style="font-size:44px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="884" cy="540" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="884" y="540" style="font-size:44px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="255" cy="650" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="255" y="650" style="font-size:44px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A created image of the same cobalt-blue enamel mug with a cream rim and rust-orange handle alone on an off-white plinth in a neutral gallery. A blank wall card and low boundary post appear nearby."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/modern-art/created/modernism-object-frame-display-v1.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M840 781 C850 746 884 734 908 758" /><ellipse class="guide-shape" cx="843" cy="570" rx="106" ry="91" /><path class="guide-line" d="M373 694 L1418 854 L1418 1010 L373 1010 Z" /><rect class="guide-shape" x="1270" y="317" width="175" height="80" rx="3" /><path class="guide-line" d="M0 708 L291 679" /><path class="guide-line" d="M968 529 L925 549" /><path class="guide-line" d="M1200 290 L1271 330" /><circle class="marker" cx="908" cy="816" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="908" y="816" style="font-size:44px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1004" cy="511" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="1004" y="511" style="font-size:44px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1245" cy="814" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="1245" y="814" style="font-size:44px">4</text><circle class="marker" cx="245" cy="657" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="245" y="657" style="font-size:44px">5</text><circle class="marker" cx="1160" cy="267" r="48" /><text class="marker-text" x="1160" y="267" style="font-size:44px">6</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>1 The lower-edge chip</strong> and <strong>2 the circular scuff</strong> recur in both created images, helping you recognize the same object. In the left image, <strong>3 the cloth and worn worktable</strong> bring use and wear forward. In the right image, <strong>4 the plinth</strong> isolates the object for attention, <strong>5 the low viewing boundary</strong> shapes the encounter, and <strong>6 the blank wall card</strong> signals that a display label could add information. These frames can make different questions available—about use, display, ownership, authorship, or collection—but they do not settle the object's status or exhaust its meaning.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>A change of frame can change the viewer's encounter with an object even while the object remains recognizable. Look for how use, display, labels, boundaries, and institutional settings direct attention—and remember that no one frame accounts for everything an object can mean.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="frame-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>Fountain by R. Mutt</em>, page from <em>The Blind Man</em></h3><p class="work-meta">May 1917 · published photograph of the lost submitted object</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="ra-question">What changes when an object is photographed, named, and published for attention?</p>
<p class="ra-dek">This is a 1917 page from <em>The Blind Man</em>, not the lost submitted object itself. It preserves Stieglitz's photograph together with printed title, credit, and exhibition statement.</p>
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Before deciding what this object is or is not, notice its changed orientation, the handwritten text, the printed title and credit, and the statement beneath the photograph. What makes this page present the object for your attention?</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" style="aspect-ratio:1800/2490" role="img" aria-label="A 1917 magazine page headed Fountain by R. Mutt and Photograph by Alfred Stieglitz. It contains a photograph of the lost submitted object, marked R. Mutt 1917, against a painted backdrop and above the printed statement The Exhibit Refused by the Independents."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/modern-art/historical/metlib-1917-the-blind-man-fountain-page.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1800 2490" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-shape" d="M300 1320 L560 1320 L560 1500 L300 1500 Z"/><path class="guide-shape" d="M360 2105 L1450 2105 L1450 2180 L360 2180 Z"/><path class="guide-shape" d="M1370 500 L1620 500 L1620 1060 L1370 1060 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="575" cy="1400" r="62"/><text class="marker-text" x="575" y="1400" style="font-size:90px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1460" cy="2140" r="62"/><text class="marker-text" x="1460" y="2140" style="font-size:90px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1650" cy="600" r="62"/><text class="marker-text" x="1650" y="600" style="font-size:90px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at what this published page records</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, "R. Mutt 1917" marks the porcelain object. At <strong>2</strong>, the printed statement calls attention to its refusal by the Independents. At <strong>3</strong>, the photograph places the object against a painted backdrop. These visible facts do not settle a universal definition of art. They show how naming, submission, photography, publication, and display can change the encounter with an ordinary manufactured thing. The original 1917 object is lost; later replicas are different historical objects.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>The way an object is titled, positioned, documented, and encountered can affect the questions a viewer asks. Context matters, but no single institution or setting automatically settles an object's value or status.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: <em>The Blind Man</em>, no. 2, May 1917, page headed "Fountain by R. Mutt," with photograph credited to Alfred Stieglitz; digitized by the Metropolitan Museum of Art Libraries. The Library's item record identifies this publication as public domain with no restrictions on use. See <a href="https://libmma.contentdm.oclc.org/digital/collection/p16028coll4/id/34701/">the Met Libraries publication record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
